# Larger sparse-stream source audit v1

## Decision

Select the anonymized UCSD malicious-URL stream for a bounded native
reproduction audit.  Do not run RCV1 merely because it is larger than 20
Newsgroups, and do not treat static webspam as the primary drift benchmark.

## Why RCV1 is not the right pressure test

RCV1-v2 is well specified: 804,414 examples, 47,236 TF-IDF features, 103 topic
labels, and the LYRL2004 chronological split of 23,149 train / 781,265 test.
The paper reports micro- and macro-F1 and explains the licensing/distribution
status of the vector files.  It is feasible under 8 GB: the reported 0.16%
density implies roughly 60.8 million nonzeros and roughly 0.73 GB for a basic
float64/int32 CSR representation.

But its source-defined learning problem is not a useful state-pressure test.
A dense float64 103-by-47,236 coefficient matrix is about 38.9 MB; training has
only 23,149 examples, while most rows are a fixed test set.  Streaming exact
micro/macro confusion counts need only per-label counters.  A coreset, hashing,
or approximate moment claim here would target a bottleneck the protocol does
not have.

## Why the URL stream is a better native application

The UCSD source provides 121 daily SVM-light files, about 2.4 million URLs and
3.2 million evolving features in a 234 MB archive.  The ICML protocol is truly
online: predict an incoming URL, observe its label, update continuously, admit
new features, and report cumulative mistakes over time.  The paper explicitly
argues that feature drift and batch memory limits motivate online learning.

The closest-work collision is unusually direct and must constrain originality:
the AISTATS 2010 follow-up already models the inverse covariance as
`D + R R^T`, evaluates rank 16 on a 20-day URL subset, preserves temporal
order, and reports a roughly 5% relative mistake reduction over diagonal CW.
This is a known mechanism, not a new low-rank proposal.  The next task is an
author-code/native-data reproduction and cost audit, not invention or a claim
of novelty.

## Resource bound and proposed first calibration

With d=3,231,961 and float64 state, one weight vector is 25,855,688 bytes;
mean plus diagonal is 51,711,376 bytes; rank-16 `R` adds 413,691,008 bytes.
The main published state is therefore about 465.4 MB plus small rank-space
matrices and parser buffers, below the 4 GiB process cap.  The raw archive must
remain under `.deps` and outside the persistent ZIP because its page does not
state an explicit redistribution license.

Before a 20-day run, retrieve and hash the author code and archive, inspect the
actual implementation against equations and data ordering, then run only a
small day-prefix calibration through `cpu_guard.py`.  Compare Perceptron, PA,
diagonal CW, CW-fact16 and a simple online logistic baseline; record cumulative
mistakes, update cost, peak RSS and new-feature handling.  Scale only if the
native definitions and measured CPU cost survive this check.

